# Story docs source

The docs **Show code** block is how people learn the API. It must be the full JSX or hook call they would paste into an app — every element the story actually renders.

- Write real usage in `render`. Do not render a helper like `<FruitSelect />` or `<DrawerExample />`.
- Do not put labels, wrappers, or layout the user needs to copy in a decorator. Put that markup in `render`.
- If a helper is unavoidable, set `parameters.docs.source.code` to the inlined usage.
- Skip `{...args}`, `fn()`, and the story object (`render:`, `decorators:`).
- Keep snippets on `docs.source.code` in sync with the live example. Never trim: include every child, option, label, wrapper, and the data those elements need. Do not drop items to “one example”, leave arrays undefined, or use `{/* ... */}`.
- Controls that hold a value (Input, Textarea, Checkbox, Switch, Slider, RadioGroup, Select, InputGroup fields) use `useState` with `value`/`checked` and a change handler. Display-only pieces (Card, Badge, Skeleton) do not.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — helper, CSF chrome, or a shortened tree
{ render: () => <FruitSelect id="select-fruit" /> }

<Select>
    <SelectTrigger placeholder="Select a fruit">{selectedLabel}</SelectTrigger>
    <SelectContent>
        <SelectOption value="apple">Apple</SelectOption>
    </SelectContent>
</Select>

// ✅ GOOD — full usage, same elements as the story
const fruits = [
    { value: "apple", label: "Apple" },
    { value: "banana", label: "Banana" },
    { value: "blueberry", label: "Blueberry" },
    { value: "grapes", label: "Grapes" },
    { value: "pineapple", label: "Pineapple" },
];
const [value, setValue] = useState<string>();
const selectedLabel = fruits.find((fruit) => fruit.value === value)?.label;

<div className="grid w-72 gap-2">
    <Label htmlFor="select-fruit">Fruit</Label>
    <Select>
        <SelectTrigger id="select-fruit" placeholder="Select a fruit">
            {selectedLabel}
        </SelectTrigger>
        <SelectContent>
            {fruits.map((fruit) => (
                <SelectOption
                    key={fruit.value}
                    value={fruit.value}
                    selected={fruit.value === value}
                    onClick={() => setValue(fruit.value)}
                >
                    {fruit.label}
                </SelectOption>
            ))}
        </SelectContent>
    </Select>
</div>
```
